{"name":"Dried Tomatoes","hex":"#ab6057","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dried","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Without water or moisture, said of something that has previously been wet or moist; resulting from the process of drying.","Usually of foods: cured, preserved by drying."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of dry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dried"},{"term":"tomatoes","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of tomato","plural of tomatoe"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tomatoes"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tomato","blocks":[{"text":"The tomato is a plant whose fruit is an edible berry that is eaten as a vegetable. The tomato is a member of the nightshade family that includes tobacco, potato, and chili peppers. It originated from western South America, and may have been domesticated there, in Mexico, or in Central America. The Spanish introduced tomatoes to Eurasia in the Columbian exchange in the 16th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/330px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomato"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/500px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/1280px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/500px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATomato_je.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopers